// 2025 GS1 (Prelims) Set A — 100 questions transcribed from 2025.docx
// Each entry: no, text, A, B, C, D, ans, category, subcategory, tags[]
export const paper = { year: 2025, name: 'GS1', set: 'A' };

export const questions = [
{no:1, text:"With reference to investments, consider the following:\nI. Bonds\nII. Hedge Funds\nIII. Stocks\nIV. Venture Capital\nHow many of the above are treated as Alternative Investment Funds (AIFs)?", A:"Only one", B:"Only two", C:"Only three", D:"All the four", ans:"B", category:"Economy", subcategory:"Financial Markets", tags:["Alternative Investment Funds","SEBI"], expl:"Hedge Funds and Venture Capital Funds can fall under the framework of Alternative Investment Funds (AIFs) regulated by SEBI. Bonds and stocks are conventional investment instruments and are not, by themselves, AIFs.", revisionPoints:"Remember the distinction:\nAIF → Privately pooled investment vehicle Hedge Funds + Venture Capital → AIF examples Stocks + Bonds → Conventional investment instruments\nAlso revise Category I, II and III AIFs, Private Equity, Mutual Funds and REITs/InvITs."},
{no:2, text:"Which of the following are the sources of income for the Reserve Bank of India?\nI. Buying and selling Government bonds\nII. Buying and selling foreign currency\nIII. Pension fund management\nIV. Lending to private companies\nV. Printing and distributing currency notes\nSelect the correct answer using the code given below.", A:"I and II only", B:"II, III and IV", C:"I, III, IV and V", D:"I, II and V", ans:"A", category:"Economy", subcategory:"Banking & Monetary Policy", tags:["RBI","Monetary Policy"], expl:"The RBI earns income from its financial operations, including buying and selling government securities and foreign currencies. Pension fund management and lending directly to private companies are not its normal sources of income. Although the RBI manages currency issuance, the cost of printing currency is an expenditure, not a source of income.", revisionPoints:"Remember:\nRBI Income → Securities + Forex operations + Interest income\nRevise RBI balance sheet, monetary policy operations, Open Market Operations (OMO), foreign exchange operations and seigniorage."},
{no:3, text:"With reference to the Government of India, consider the following information:\nOrganization — Function — Works under\nI. Directorate of Enforcement — Enforcement of the Fugitive Economic Offenders Act, 2018 — Internal Security Division-I, Ministry of Home Affairs\nII. Directorate of Revenue Intelligence — Enforces the provisions of the Customs Act, 1962 — Department of Revenue, Ministry of Finance\nIII. Directorate General of Systems and Data Management — Carries out big data analytics to assist tax officers and policy — Department of Revenue, Ministry of Finance\nIn how many of the above rows is the information correctly matched?", A:"Only one", B:"Only two", C:"All the three", D:"None", ans:"A", category:"Polity & Governance", subcategory:"Government Bodies", tags:["Enforcement Directorate","Ministry of Finance"], expl:"Row II is correctly matched. The Directorate of Revenue Intelligence (DRI) functions under the Department of Revenue, Ministry of Finance, and deals with enforcement of customs and anti-smuggling laws.\nThe Directorate of Enforcement does not work under the Ministry of Home Affairs; it functions under the Department of Revenue, Ministry of Finance. The Directorate General of Systems and Data Management is also associated with the indirect tax administration framework, but the given matching is not correct in the manner stated.", revisionPoints:"Remember:\nED → Department of Revenue → Ministry of Finance DRI → Department of Revenue → Ministry of Finance\nAlso revise ED, DRI, DGGI, CBI, NIA, FIU-IND and their parent ministries/functions."},
{no:4, text:"Consider the following statements:\nStatement I: The Reserve Bank of India mandates all the listed companies in India to submit a Business Responsibility and Sustainability Report (BRSR).\nStatement II: In India, a company submitting a BRSR makes disclosures in the report that are largely non-financial in nature.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?", A:"I only", B:"II only", C:"Both I and II", D:"Neither I nor II", ans:"B", category:"Economy", subcategory:"Corporate Regulation", tags:["BRSR","SEBI","ESG"], expl:"BRSR is associated with SEBI, not the RBI. It is a sustainability-related disclosure framework for listed entities. BRSR disclosures largely cover environmental, social and governance (ESG) aspects and are therefore predominantly non-financial in nature.", revisionPoints:"Remember:\nBRSR → SEBI → ESG disclosures\nAlso revise ESG, BRSR Core, sustainability reporting and SEBI's role in corporate disclosures."},
{no:5, text:"Consider the following statements:\nStatement I: In India, income from allied agricultural activities like poultry farming and wool rearing in rural areas is exempted from any tax.\nStatement II: In India, rural agricultural land is not considered a capital asset under the provisions of the Income-tax Act, 1961.\nWhich one of the following is correct in respect of the above statements?", A:"Both Statement I and Statement II are correct and Statement II explains Statement I.", B:"Both Statement I and Statement II are correct but Statement II does not explain Statement I.", C:"Statement I is correct but Statement II is not correct.", D:"Statement I is not correct but Statement II is correct.", ans:"D", category:"Economy", subcategory:"Taxation", tags:["Income Tax","Agriculture Income"], expl:"Income from activities such as poultry farming and wool rearing is not automatically treated as agricultural income merely because it is carried out in rural areas. Therefore, Statement I is incorrect.\nRural agricultural land, subject to the conditions specified under the Income-tax Act, is generally excluded from the definition of a capital asset. Hence Statement II is correct.", revisionPoints:"Remember:\nAgricultural income ≠ Every rural income\nAlso revise agricultural income, allied activities, rural agricultural land, capital assets and taxation of agricultural income."},
{no:6, text:"Consider the following statements:\nI. India has joined the Minerals Security Partnership as a member.\nII. India is a resource-rich country in all the 30 critical minerals that it has identified.\nIII. The Parliament in 2023 amended the Mines and Minerals (Development and Regulation) Act, 1957 empowering the Central Government to exclusively auction mining leases and composite licences for certain critical minerals.\nWhich of the statements given above are correct?", A:"I and II only", B:"II and III only", C:"I and III only", D:"I, II and III", ans:"C", category:"Economy", subcategory:"Mining & Minerals", tags:["Critical Minerals","MMDR Act","Minerals Security Partnership"], expl:"India joined the Minerals Security Partnership (MSP). However, India is not resource-rich in all the critical minerals it has identified and remains dependent on imports for several of them.\nThe 2023 amendment to the MMDR Act gave the Central Government powers relating to auction of mining leases and composite licences for specified critical minerals.", revisionPoints:"Remember:\nCritical Minerals → Economic + Strategic Security\nAlso revise India's critical mineral list, Minerals Security Partnership, MMDR Act 2023 and mineral import dependence."},
{no:7, text:"Consider the following statements:\nStatement I: As regards returns from an investment in a company, generally, bondholders are considered to be relatively at lower risk than stockholders.\nStatement II: Bondholders are lenders to a company whereas stockholders are its owners.\nStatement III: For repayment purpose, bondholders are prioritized over stockholders by a company.\nWhich one of the following is correct in respect of the above statements?", A:"Both Statement II and Statement III are correct and both of them explain Statement I.", B:"Both Statement I and Statement II are correct and Statement I explains Statement II.", C:"Only one of the Statements II and III is correct and that explains Statement I.", D:"Neither Statement II nor Statement III is correct.", ans:"A", category:"Economy", subcategory:"Financial Markets", tags:["Bonds","Stock Market"], expl:"Bondholders are creditors/lenders of the company, whereas stockholders are owners. In case of liquidation, creditors generally have priority over equity shareholders for repayment. Therefore, bonds generally carry lower risk than stocks, although the exact risk depends on the type of security.", revisionPoints:"Remember the hierarchy:\nBondholder → Lender/Creditor → Higher repayment priority Stockholder → Owner → Residual claim\nAlso revise Debt vs Equity, risk-return relationship, secured/unsecured bonds and liquidation priority."},
{no:8, text:"Consider the following statements:\nI. India accounts for a very large portion of all equity option contracts traded globally.\nII. India's stock market has grown rapidly in the recent past even overtaking Hong Kong's at some point of time.\nIII. There is no regulatory body either to warn small investors about the risks of options trading or to act on unregistered financial advisors in this regard.\nWhich of the statements given above are correct?", A:"I and II only", B:"II and III only", C:"I and III only", D:"I, II and III", ans:"A", category:"Economy", subcategory:"Financial Markets", tags:["Stock Market","SEBI"], expl:"India has become one of the world's largest markets for equity derivatives/options trading, and the Indian stock market has grown significantly, at times surpassing Hong Kong in market capitalization.\nStatement III is incorrect because SEBI regulates India's securities market and has measures relating to investor protection and unregistered financial advisers.", revisionPoints:"Remember:\nSEBI → Securities Market Regulator\nAlso revise Futures vs Options, derivatives, options trading risks, SEBI and investor protection mechanisms."},
{no:9, text:"Consider the following statements:\nStatement I: Circular economy reduces the emissions of greenhouse gases.\nStatement II: Circular economy reduces the use of raw materials as inputs.\nStatement III: Circular economy reduces wastage in the production process.\nWhich one of the following is correct in respect of the above statements?", A:"Both Statement II and Statement III are correct and both of them explain Statement I.", B:"Both Statement II and Statement III are correct but only one of them explains Statement I.", C:"Only one of the Statements II and III is correct and that explains Statement I.", D:"Neither Statement II nor Statement III is correct.", ans:"B", category:"Environment & Ecology", subcategory:"Sustainable Development", tags:["Circular Economy"], expl:"A circular economy aims to keep resources and products in use for as long as possible through reuse, repair, refurbishment and recycling.\nIt reduces the demand for new raw materials and reduces waste. Both can contribute to lower greenhouse-gas emissions, but the direct relationship between reduced raw-material extraction and emissions is stronger, while reduced production wastage does not necessarily explain the emissions reduction in every case.", revisionPoints:"Remember the basic contrast:\nLinear Economy → Take → Make → Use → Dispose Circular Economy → Reduce → Reuse → Repair → Recycle\nAlso revise resource efficiency, waste management, 3R/5R, sustainable production and climate change."},
{no:10, text:"Consider the following statements:\nI. Capital receipts create a liability or cause a reduction in the assets of the Government.\nII. Borrowings and disinvestment are capital receipts.\nIII. Interest received on loans creates a liability of the Government.\nWhich of the statements given above are correct?", A:"I and II only", B:"II and III only", C:"I and III only", D:"I, II and III", ans:"A", category:"Economy", subcategory:"Public Finance", tags:["Budget","Capital Receipts"], expl:"Capital receipts either create a liability for the government or reduce its assets. Borrowings create a liability, while disinvestment reduces the government's ownership/assets.\nInterest received on loans is a revenue receipt, and it does not create a liability for the government.", revisionPoints:"Remember:\nCapital Receipt → Creates Liability OR Reduces Asset\nBorrowing → Liability ↑ Disinvestment → Asset ↓ Interest received → Revenue Receipt\nAlso revise Capital vs Revenue Receipts, Fiscal Deficit, Government Borrowing and Disinvestment."},
{no:11, text:"Consider the following statements about Raja Ram Mohan Roy:\nI. He possessed great love and respect for the traditional philosophical systems of the East.\nII. He desired his countrymen to accept the rational and scientific approach and the principle of human dignity and social equality of all men and women.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?", A:"I only", B:"II only", C:"Both I and II", D:"Neither I nor II", ans:"C", category:"History", subcategory:"Modern India", tags:["Raja Ram Mohan Roy","Reform Movements"], expl:"Raja Ram Mohan Roy combined respect for India's traditional philosophical and religious traditions with an emphasis on reason, rationalism and modern scientific thought. He strongly advocated human dignity, social equality and reform of social practices, including the abolition of Sati.", revisionPoints:"Remember:\nRaja Ram Mohan Roy → Tradition + Reason + Social Reform\nAlso revise Brahmo Samaj, abolition of Sati, women's rights, modern education and his role in the Bengal Renaissance."},
{no:12, text:"Consider the following subjects with regard to the Non-Cooperation Programme:\nI. Boycott of law courts and foreign cloth\nII. Observance of strict non-violence\nIII. Retention of titles and honours without using them in public\nIV. Establishment of Panchayats for settling disputes\nHow many of the above were parts of the Non-Cooperation Programme?", A:"Only one", B:"Only two", C:"Only three", D:"All the four", ans:"C", category:"History", subcategory:"Modern India", tags:["Non-Cooperation Movement"], expl:"The Non-Cooperation Programme included boycott of law courts and foreign cloth, strict adherence to non-violence, and establishment of panchayats for settling disputes. However, the programme called for the surrender of titles and honours, not their retention.", revisionPoints:"Remember the Non-Cooperation approach:\nBoycott → Schools + Colleges + Courts + Foreign Cloth Surrender → Titles and honours Constructive → Panchayats + Khadi + National institutions Foundation → Strict Non-violence\nAlso revise Gandhi's Non-Cooperation Movement, Khilafat Movement, Chauri Chaura and withdrawal of the movement."},
{no:13, text:"The irrigation device called 'Araghatta' was", A:"A water bag made of leather pulled over a pulley", B:"A large wheel with earthen pots tied to the outer ends of its spokes", C:"A large earthen pot driven by bullocks", D:"A large water bucket pulled up by rope directly by hand", ans:"B", category:"History", subcategory:"Ancient India", tags:["Ancient Technology","Irrigation"], expl:"The Araghatta was a traditional water-lifting device consisting of a large wheel fitted with pots. As the wheel rotated, the pots lifted water from a lower level and discharged it at a higher level for irrigation.", revisionPoints:"Remember:\nAraghatta → Wheel + Earthen Pots → Water Lifting\nAlso revise traditional irrigation systems, ancient water-management techniques, wells, tanks and agricultural technology in early India."},
{no:14, text:"Who among the following rulers in ancient India had assumed the titles 'Mattavilasa', 'Vichitrachitta' and 'Gunabhara'?", A:"Mahendravarman I", B:"Simhavishnu", C:"Narasimhavarman I", D:"Simhavarman", ans:"A", category:"History", subcategory:"Ancient India", tags:["Pallavas"], expl:"Mahendravarman I, the Pallava ruler, was a patron of art, architecture and literature. He used titles such as Mattavilasa, Vichitrachitta and Gunabhara. He is also associated with the early development of Pallava rock-cut architecture.", revisionPoints:"Remember:\nMahendravarman I → Pallava → Rock-cut architecture + Literature + Art\nAlso revise Pallavas, Narasimhavarman I, Mamallapuram, Pancha Rathas and Shore Temple."},
{no:15, text:"Fa-hien (Faxian), the Chinese pilgrim, travelled to India during the reign of", A:"Samudragupta", B:"Chandragupta II", C:"Kumaragupta I", D:"Skandagupta", ans:"B", category:"History", subcategory:"Ancient India", tags:["Gupta Empire","Chinese Travellers"], expl:"The Chinese Buddhist pilgrim Fa-hien (Faxian) travelled to India during the reign of Chandragupta II of the Gupta dynasty, around the early fifth century CE. He came primarily to study and collect Buddhist texts and also recorded observations about Indian society and administration.", revisionPoints:"Remember this sequence:\nFa-hien → Chandragupta II → Gupta Period\nAlso revise Chinese travellers in India: Fa-hien, Xuanzang and Yijing, along with the rulers and periods associated with them."},
{no:16, text:"Who among the following led a successful military campaign against the kingdom of Srivijaya, the powerful maritime State, which ruled the Malay Peninsula, Sumatra, Java and the neighbouring islands?", A:"Amoghavarsha (Rashtrakuta)", B:"Prataparudra (Kakatiya)", C:"Rajendra I (Chola)", D:"Vishnuvardhana (Hoysala)", ans:"C", category:"History", subcategory:"Medieval India", tags:["Chola Empire","Srivijaya"], expl:"Rajendra I, the Chola ruler, launched a major naval expedition against Srivijaya in Southeast Asia. The campaign demonstrated the Cholas' strong naval capabilities and maritime commercial interests.", revisionPoints:"Remember:\nRajendra I → Chola → Naval Expedition → Srivijaya\nAlso revise Chola administration, naval power, temple architecture, overseas trade and Southeast Asian connections."},
{no:17, text:"With reference to ancient India (600–322 BC), consider the following pairs:\nTerritorial Region — River flowing in the region\nI. Asmaka — Godavari\nII. Kamboja — Vipas\nIII. Avanti — Mahanadi\nIV. Kosala — Sarayu\nHow many of the pairs given above are correctly matched?", A:"Only one", B:"Only two", C:"Only three", D:"All the four", ans:"B", category:"History", subcategory:"Ancient India", tags:["Ancient Geography","Mahajanapadas"], expl:"The correctly matched pairs are:\nAsmaka — Godavari ✅\nKosala — Sarayu ✅\nKamboja is not associated with the Vipas (Beas) in this context, while Avanti was associated primarily with the Narmada, not the Mahanadi.", revisionPoints:"Remember these Mahajanapada associations:\nAsmaka → Godavari Kosala → Sarayu Avanti → Narmada\nAlso revise the 16 Mahajanapadas, their locations, capitals and important rivers."},
{no:18, text:"The first Gandharva Mahavidyalaya, a music training school, was set up in 1901 by Vishnu Digambar Paluskar in", A:"Delhi", B:"Gwalior", C:"Ujjain", D:"Lahore", ans:"D", category:"Art & Culture", subcategory:"Music", tags:["Indian Classical Music"], expl:"Vishnu Digambar Paluskar, an important figure in the revival and institutionalisation of Indian classical music, established the first Gandharva Mahavidyalaya in Lahore in 1901.", revisionPoints:"Remember:\nVishnu Digambar Paluskar → Gandharva Mahavidyalaya → Lahore → 1901\nAlso revise Hindustani classical music, Carnatic music, important musicians and institutions associated with India's cultural history."},
{no:19, text:"Ashokan inscriptions suggest that the 'Pradeshika', 'Rajuka' and 'Yukta' were important officers at the", A:"Village-level administration", B:"District-level administration", C:"Provincial administration", D:"Level of the central administration", ans:"B", category:"History", subcategory:"Ancient India", tags:["Mauryan Administration","Ashoka"], expl:"The Pradeshika, Rajuka and Yukta were important officials associated with district-level administration during the Mauryan period. Ashoka's inscriptions provide valuable information about their administrative responsibilities.", revisionPoints:"Remember:\nPradeshika + Rajuka + Yukta → District Administration\nAlso revise Mauryan administration, Ashokan Dhamma, inscriptions, officers and the nature of the Mauryan state."},
{no:20, text:"Consider the following statements in respect of the Non-Cooperation Movement:\nI. The Congress declared the attainment of 'Swaraj' by all legitimate and peaceful means to be its objective.\nII. It was to be implemented in stages with civil disobedience and non-payment of taxes for the next stage only if 'Swaraj' did not come within a year and the Government resorted to repression.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?", A:"I only", B:"II only", C:"Both I and II", D:"Neither I nor II", ans:"C", category:"History", subcategory:"Modern India", tags:["Non-Cooperation Movement"], expl:"The Congress adopted Swaraj through legitimate and peaceful means as the objective of the Non-Cooperation programme. The movement was planned in stages, with civil disobedience and non-payment of taxes envisaged as a later stage if the desired result was not achieved and repression continued.", revisionPoints:"Remember the progression:\nNon-Cooperation → Boycott → Constructive Work → Civil Disobedience (next stage)\nAlso revise the Nagpur Session (1920), Swaraj, Khilafat–Non-Cooperation alliance, Chauri Chaura and withdrawal of the movement."},
{no:21, text:"Consider the following countries:\nI. Austria\nII. Bulgaria\nIII. Croatia\nIV. Serbia\nV. Sweden\nVI. North Macedonia\nHow many of the above are members of the North Atlantic Treaty Organization (NATO)?", A:"Only three", B:"Only four", C:"Only five", D:"All the six", ans:"B", category:"International Relations", subcategory:"Organizations", tags:["NATO"], expl:"Among the six countries, Bulgaria, Croatia, Sweden and North Macedonia are NATO members. Austria and Serbia are not NATO members.", revisionPoints:"Remember:\nNATO members → Bulgaria + Croatia + Sweden + North Macedonia\nAlso revise NATO, EU, European non-NATO countries and NATO's expansion in Europe."},
{no:22, text:"Consider the following countries:\nI. Bolivia\nII. Brazil\nIII. Colombia\nIV. Ecuador\nV. Paraguay\nVI. Venezuela\nAndes Mountains pass through how many of the above countries?", A:"Only two", B:"Only three", C:"Only four", D:"Only five", ans:"C", category:"Geography", subcategory:"Physical Geography", tags:["Andes","World Geography"], expl:"The Andes Mountains pass through Bolivia, Colombia, Ecuador and Venezuela among the countries listed. They do not pass through Brazil or Paraguay.", revisionPoints:"Remember:\nAndes → Venezuela → Colombia → Ecuador → Peru → Bolivia → Chile → Argentina\nAlso revise major mountain ranges, their countries and important geographical features of South America."},
{no:23, text:"Consider the following water bodies:\nI. Lake Tanganyika\nII. Lake Tonlé Sap\nIII. Patos Lagoon\nThrough how many of them does the equator pass?", A:"Only one", B:"Only two", C:"All the three", D:"None", ans:"D", category:"Geography", subcategory:"Physical Geography", tags:["Equator","Lakes"], expl:"The Equator does not pass through any of these three water bodies. Lake Tanganyika lies south of the Equator, Tonlé Sap is in Cambodia, and Patos Lagoon is in southern Brazil.", revisionPoints:"Remember:\nEquator → 0° latitude\nAlso revise countries crossed by the Equator, Tropic of Cancer, Tropic of Capricorn and major lakes crossed/near major latitudes."},
{no:24, text:"Consider the following statements about turmeric during the year 2022–23:\nI. India is the largest producer and exporter of turmeric in the world.\nII. More than 30 varieties of turmeric are grown in India.\nIII. Maharashtra, Telangana, Karnataka and Tamil Nadu are major turmeric-producing States in India.\nWhich of the statements given above are correct?", A:"I and II only", B:"II and III only", C:"I and III only", D:"I, II and III", ans:"C", category:"Geography", subcategory:"Economic Geography", tags:["Agriculture","Turmeric","India Geography"], expl:"India is a major global producer and exporter of turmeric, and several Indian states are important producers. However, the statement that more than 30 varieties are grown in India is not correct in the context of the question.", revisionPoints:"Remember:\nTurmeric → India → Major producer + exporter\nAlso revise major spice-producing states, GI-tagged agricultural products and India's spice exports."},
{no:25, text:"Which of the following are the evidences of the phenomenon of continental drift?\nI. The belt of ancient rocks from Brazil coast matches with those from Western Africa.\nII. The gold deposits of Ghana are derived from the Brazil plateau when the two continents lay side by side.\nIII. The Gondwana system of sediments from India is known to have its counterparts in six different landmasses of the Southern Hemisphere.\nSelect the correct answer using the code given below.", A:"I and III only", B:"I and II only", C:"I, II and III", D:"II and III only", ans:"C", category:"Geography", subcategory:"Physical Geography", tags:["Continental Drift","Plate Tectonics"], expl:"The matching geological formations across continents, similarities in mineral deposits and the distribution of Gondwana sediments across southern landmasses are important evidence supporting continental drift.", revisionPoints:"Remember the major evidence:\nContinental Drift → Jigsaw fit + Rocks + Minerals + Fossils + Paleoclimate\nAlso revise Wegener, Pangaea, Laurasia, Gondwana and Plate Tectonics."},
{no:26, text:"Consider the following statements:\nStatement I: The amount of dust particles in the atmosphere is more in subtropical and temperate areas than in equatorial and polar regions.\nStatement II: Subtropical and temperate areas have less dry winds.\nWhich one of the following is correct in respect of the above statements?", A:"Both Statement I and Statement II are correct and Statement II explains Statement I.", B:"Both Statement I and Statement II are correct but Statement II does not explain Statement I.", C:"Statement I is correct but Statement II is not correct.", D:"Statement I is not correct but Statement II is correct.", ans:"C", category:"Geography", subcategory:"Physical Geography", tags:["Atmosphere","Climatology"], expl:"Dust particles are relatively abundant in subtropical and temperate regions, partly because of dry conditions, deserts and strong winds. Statement II is incorrect because these regions generally experience more dry winds, not less.", revisionPoints:"Remember:\nDry regions + Strong winds → More atmospheric dust\nAlso revise atmospheric aerosols, dust, humidity, winds and their effects on climate."},
{no:27, text:"Consider the following statements:\nStatement I: In January, in the Northern Hemisphere, the isotherms bend equatorward while crossing the landmasses, and poleward while crossing the oceans.\nStatement II: In January, the air over the oceans is warmer than that over the landmasses in the Northern Hemisphere.\nWhich one of the following is correct in respect of the above statements?", A:"Both Statement I and Statement II are correct and Statement II explains Statement I.", B:"Both Statement I and Statement II are correct but Statement II does not explain Statement I.", C:"Statement I is correct but Statement II is not correct.", D:"Statement I is not correct but Statement II is correct.", ans:"A", category:"Geography", subcategory:"Physical Geography", tags:["Isotherms","Climatology"], expl:"In January, it is winter in the Northern Hemisphere. Land cools more rapidly than water, while oceans retain heat. Therefore, land is colder and oceans are relatively warmer, causing isotherms to bend equatorward over land and poleward over oceans.", revisionPoints:"Remember:\nJanuary → NH Winter → Land colder → Ocean warmer\nAlso revise isotherms, continentality, maritime influence and seasonal temperature distribution."},
{no:28, text:"Consider the following statements:\nStatement I: In the context of effect of water on rocks, chalk is known as a very permeable rock whereas clay is known as quite an impermeable or least permeable rock.\nStatement II: Chalk is porous and hence can absorb water.\nStatement III: Clay is not at all porous.\nWhich one of the following is correct in respect of the above statements?", A:"Both Statement II and Statement III are correct and both of them explain Statement I.", B:"Both Statement II and Statement III are correct but only one of them explains Statement I.", C:"Only one of the Statements II and III is correct and that explains Statement I.", D:"Neither Statement II nor Statement III is correct.", ans:"C", category:"Geography", subcategory:"Physical Geography", tags:["Rocks","Geomorphology"], expl:"Chalk has interconnected pores through which water can pass, making it relatively permeable. Clay can contain pores but its very fine particles make the pores extremely small, so water moves through it very slowly. Therefore, saying that clay is not at all porous is incorrect.", revisionPoints:"Remember:\nPorosity ≠ Permeability\nChalk → Porous + Permeable Clay → Porous but Very Low Permeability"},
{no:29, text:"Consider the following statements:\nI. Without the atmosphere, temperature would be well below the freezing point everywhere on the Earth's surface.\nII. Heat absorbed and trapped by the atmosphere maintains our planet's average temperature.\nIII. Atmospheric gases, like carbon dioxide, are particularly good at absorbing and trapping radiation.\nWhich of the statements given above are correct?", A:"I and III only", B:"I and II only", C:"I, II and III", D:"II and III only", ans:"C", category:"Environment & Ecology", subcategory:"Climate Change", tags:["Greenhouse Effect","Climate Change"], expl:"The atmosphere helps regulate Earth's temperature through the greenhouse effect. Greenhouse gases such as carbon dioxide absorb outgoing infrared radiation and help maintain a temperature suitable for life.", revisionPoints:"Remember:\nAtmosphere → Greenhouse effect → Heat retention → Habitable Earth\nAlso revise greenhouse gases, Earth's energy balance, albedo and global warming."},
{no:30, text:"Consider the following statements about the Rashtriya Gokul Mission:\nI. It is important for the upliftment of the rural poor as majority of low producing indigenous animals are with small and marginal farmers and landless labourers.\nII. It was initiated to promote indigenous cattle and buffalo rearing and conservation in a scientific and holistic manner.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?", A:"I only", B:"II only", C:"Both I and II", D:"Neither I nor II", ans:"B", category:"Economy", subcategory:"Agriculture & Allied", tags:["Rashtriya Gokul Mission","Livestock"], expl:"The Rashtriya Gokul Mission focuses on the development, conservation and genetic improvement of indigenous cattle breeds. Statement I is incorrect as framed, while Statement II correctly describes the purpose of the mission.", revisionPoints:"Remember:\nRashtriya Gokul Mission → Indigenous cattle → Conservation + Genetic improvement\nAlso revise animal husbandry schemes, livestock breeds, dairy development and indigenous cattle conservation."},
{no:31, text:"Consider the following statements:\nStatement I: Studies indicate that carbon dioxide emissions from the cement industry account for more than 5% of global carbon emissions.\nStatement II: Silica-bearing clay is mixed with limestone while manufacturing cement.\nStatement III: Limestone is converted into lime during clinker production for cement manufacturing.\nWhich one of the following is correct in respect of the above statements?", A:"Both Statement II and Statement III are correct and both of them explain Statement I.", B:"Both Statement II and Statement III are correct but only one of them explains Statement I.", C:"Only one of the Statements II and III is correct and that explains Statement I.", D:"Neither Statement II nor Statement III is correct.", ans:"B", category:"Environment & Ecology", subcategory:"Climate Change", tags:["Cement Industry","Carbon Emissions"], expl:"Cement production uses limestone and silica-bearing materials such as clay. During clinker production, limestone undergoes calcination and releases carbon dioxide while forming lime. This process contributes significantly to the industry's emissions.", revisionPoints:"Remember:\nCement → Limestone + Clay → Clinker → CO₂ emissions\nAlso revise industrial emissions, decarbonisation of cement, carbon capture and low-carbon cement technologies."},
{no:32, text:"Consider the following statements:\nStatement I: At the 28th United Nations Climate Change Conference (COP28), India refrained from signing the Declaration on Climate and Health.\nStatement II: The COP28 Declaration on Climate and Health is a binding declaration; and if signed, it becomes mandatory to decarbonize the health sector.\nStatement III: If India's health sector is decarbonized, the resilience of its healthcare system may be compromised.\nWhich one of the following is correct in respect of the above statements?", A:"Both Statement II and Statement III are correct and both of them explain Statement I.", B:"Both Statement II and Statement III are correct but only one of them explains Statement I.", C:"Only one of the Statements II and III is correct and that explains Statement I.", D:"Neither Statement II nor Statement III is correct.", ans:"D", category:"Environment & Ecology", subcategory:"Climate Change", tags:["COP28","Climate Change"], expl:"The COP28 Declaration on Climate and Health was not legally binding. Signing it did not create a mandatory legal obligation to decarbonize the health sector. Decarbonisation of healthcare can also contribute to resilience rather than necessarily compromising it. Hence Statements II and III are incorrect.", revisionPoints:"Remember:\nClimate + Health → Heat + Diseases + Healthcare resilience + Emissions\nAlso revise COP28, climate-health nexus, climate-resilient healthcare and international climate declarations."},
{no:33, text:"Consider the following statements:\nStatement I: Scientific studies suggest that a shift is taking place in the Earth's rotation axis.\nStatement II: Solar flares and associated coronal mass ejections bombarded the Earth's outermost atmosphere with tremendous amount of energy.\nStatement III: As the Earth's polar ice melts, the water tends to move towards the equator.\nWhich one of the following is correct in respect of the above statements?", A:"Both Statement II and Statement III are correct and both of them explain Statement I.", B:"Both Statement II and Statement III are correct but only one of them explains Statement I.", C:"Only one of the Statements II and III is correct and that explains Statement I.", D:"Neither Statement II nor Statement III is correct.", ans:"B", category:"Geography", subcategory:"Physical Geography", tags:["Earth's Axis","Geophysics"], expl:"Changes in the distribution of Earth's mass can influence its rotation and the position of its rotational axis. Melting polar ice redistributes water toward lower latitudes and can affect Earth's rotation. Solar activity does not provide the explanation for this particular shift.", revisionPoints:"Remember:\nEarth's rotation → Mass distribution matters\nAlso revise Earth's rotation, polar ice melt, sea-level rise, mass redistribution and Earth's axis."},
{no:34, text:"Consider the following statements:\nStatement I: Article 6 of the Paris Agreement on climate change is frequently discussed in global discussions on sustainable development and climate change.\nStatement II: Article 6 of the Paris Agreement on climate change sets out the principles of carbon markets.\nStatement III: Article 6 of the Paris Agreement on climate change intends to promote inter-country non-market strategies to reach their climate targets.\nWhich one of the following is correct in respect of the above statements?", A:"Both Statement II and Statement III are correct and both of them explain Statement I.", B:"Both Statement II and Statement III are correct but only one of them explains Statement I.", C:"Only one of the Statements II and III is correct and that explains Statement I.", D:"Neither Statement II nor Statement III is correct.", ans:"C", category:"Environment & Ecology", subcategory:"Climate Change", tags:["Paris Agreement","Carbon Markets"], expl:"Article 6 provides a framework for international cooperation in achieving climate goals, including market and non-market approaches. Statement II is broadly correct because Article 6 covers mechanisms related to international carbon markets. Statement III is incorrect because it does not accurately describe the specific scope of Article 6.", revisionPoints:"Remember:\nParis Agreement Article 6 → International cooperation\nAlso revise Article 6.2, Article 6.4, Article 6.8, carbon markets, carbon credits and non-market approaches."},
{no:35, text:"Which one of the following launched the 'Nature Solutions Finance Hub for Asia and the Pacific'?", A:"The Asian Development Bank (ADB)", B:"The Asian Infrastructure Investment Bank (AIIB)", C:"The New Development Bank (NDB)", D:"The International Bank for Reconstruction and Development (IBRD)", ans:"A", category:"Environment & Ecology", subcategory:"International Environmental Bodies", tags:["ADB","Nature Finance"], expl:"The Asian Development Bank (ADB) launched the Nature Solutions Finance Hub for Asia and the Pacific to help mobilize finance for nature-positive investments and address biodiversity and climate-related challenges.", revisionPoints:"Remember:\nNature Solutions Finance Hub → ADB → Asia & Pacific\nAlso revise multilateral development banks, ADB, AIIB, NDB and environmental financing mechanisms."},
{no:36, text:"With reference to 'Direct Air Capture', an emerging technology, which of the following statements is/are correct?\nI. It can be used as a way of carbon sequestration.\nII. It can be a valuable approach for plastic production and in food processing.\nIII. In aviation, it can be a source of carbon for combining with hydrogen to create synthetic low-carbon fuel.\nSelect the correct answer using the code given below.", A:"I and II only", B:"III only", C:"I, II and III", D:"None of the above statements is correct.", ans:"C", category:"Science & Technology", subcategory:"Environmental Technology", tags:["Direct Air Capture","Carbon Capture"], expl:"Direct Air Capture (DAC) removes carbon dioxide directly from atmospheric air. Captured CO₂ can be stored for carbon sequestration or used as a feedstock in industries such as food processing and plastics. It can also be combined with hydrogen to produce synthetic fuels, including fuels relevant to aviation.", revisionPoints:"Remember:\nDAC → Capture CO₂ from air → Store OR Reuse\nAlso revise carbon capture and storage (CCS), carbon capture and utilisation (CCU), green hydrogen and synthetic fuels."},
{no:37, text:"Regarding Peacock tarantula (Gooty tarantula), consider the following statements:\nI. It is an omnivorous crustacean.\nII. Its natural habitat in India is only limited to some forest areas.\nIII. In its natural habitat, it is an arboreal species.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?", A:"I only", B:"I and III", C:"II only", D:"II and III", ans:"D", category:"Environment & Ecology", subcategory:"Biodiversity", tags:["Gooty Tarantula","Species in News"], expl:"The Peacock tarantula, also known as the Gooty tarantula, is a spider, not a crustacean. It has a restricted natural distribution in certain forest habitats of India and is arboreal, meaning it lives primarily in trees.", revisionPoints:"Remember:\nGooty Tarantula → Spider → Arboreal → Restricted Indian habitat\nAlso revise endemic species, IUCN status, threatened species and biodiversity hotspots of India."},
{no:38, text:"Consider the following statements:\nI. Carbon dioxide (CO₂) emissions in India are less than 0.5 t CO₂/capita.\nII. In terms of CO₂ emissions from fuel combustion, India ranks second in the Asia-Pacific region.\nIII. Electricity and heat producers are the largest sources of CO₂ emissions in India.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?", A:"I and III only", B:"II only", C:"II and III only", D:"I, II and III", ans:"C", category:"Environment & Ecology", subcategory:"Climate Change", tags:["Carbon Emissions","India"], expl:"India's per-capita CO₂ emissions are well above 0.5 tonnes, making Statement I incorrect. India is a major emitter in the Asia-Pacific region, and electricity and heat production constitute a major source of CO₂ emissions.", revisionPoints:"Remember:\nIndia → High total emissions but comparatively lower per-capita emissions than many developed countries\nAlso revise per-capita vs total emissions, energy sector emissions and India's climate commitments."},
{no:39, text:"Consider the following pairs:\nPlant — Description\nI. Cassava — Woody shrub\nII. Ginger — Herb with pseudostem\nIII. Malabar spinach — Herbaceous climber\nIV. Mint — Annual shrub\nV. Papaya — Woody shrub\nHow many of the above pairs are correctly matched?", A:"Only two", B:"Only three", C:"Only four", D:"All the five", ans:"B", category:"Environment & Ecology", subcategory:"Biodiversity", tags:["Plants","Botany"], expl:"Cassava, ginger and Malabar spinach are correctly described in the given pairs. Mint is generally a perennial herb, not an annual shrub. Papaya is a large herbaceous plant, not a woody shrub.", revisionPoints:"Remember:\nCassava → Woody shrub Ginger → Herbaceous plant with pseudostem Malabar spinach → Climber Mint → Perennial herb Papaya → Large herbaceous plant\nAlso revise botanical characteristics of common crops, plant morphology and economic botany."},
{no:40, text:"With reference to the planet Earth, consider the following statements:\nI. Rain forests produce more oxygen than that produced by oceans.\nII. Marine phytoplankton and photosynthetic bacteria produce about 50% of the world's oxygen.\nIII. Well-oxygenated surface water contains several folds higher oxygen than that in atmospheric air.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?", A:"I and II", B:"II only", C:"I and III", D:"None of the above statements is correct.", ans:"B", category:"Environment & Ecology", subcategory:"Biodiversity", tags:["Oxygen","Marine Ecology"], expl:"Marine phytoplankton and photosynthetic bacteria are responsible for roughly half of global oxygen production. Therefore, Statement II is correct.\nRainforests do not produce more oxygen than oceans, and oxygen concentration in water is much lower than in the atmosphere, making Statements I and III incorrect.", revisionPoints:"Remember:\nOceans → Phytoplankton → Major share of global oxygen production\nAlso revise phytoplankton, marine ecosystems, ocean productivity, carbon cycle and oxygen cycle."},
{no:41, text:"Consider the following types of vehicles:\nI. Full battery electric vehicles\nII. Hydrogen fuel cell vehicles\nIII. Fuel cell-electric hybrid vehicles\nHow many of the above are considered as alternative powertrain vehicles?", A:"Only one", B:"Only two", C:"All the three", D:"None", ans:"C", category:"Science & Technology", subcategory:"Automobile Technology", tags:["Electric Vehicles","Alternative Fuel Vehicles"], expl:"Alternative powertrain vehicles use propulsion systems other than conventional internal-combustion engines. Battery electric, hydrogen fuel-cell and fuel-cell electric hybrid vehicles are all alternative powertrain vehicles.", revisionPoints:"Remember:\nAlternative Powertrain → Battery Electric + Hydrogen Fuel Cell + Fuel Cell Hybrid\nAlso revise BEV, HEV, PHEV, hydrogen fuel-cell vehicles and conventional internal-combustion engines."},
{no:42, text:"With reference to Unmanned Aerial Vehicles (UAVs), consider the following statements:\nI. All types of UAVs can do vertical landing.\nII. All types of UAVs can do automated hovering.\nIII. All types of UAVs can use battery only as a source of power supply.\nHow many of the statements given above are correct?", A:"Only one", B:"Only two", C:"All the three", D:"None", ans:"A", category:"Science & Technology", subcategory:"Defence Technology", tags:["UAV","Drones"], expl:"Not all UAVs can perform vertical landing or automated hovering. These capabilities depend on the UAV's design, such as whether it is a multirotor, fixed-wing or hybrid UAV. UAVs can also use different power sources, so batteries are not the only option.", revisionPoints:"Remember:\nUAV types → Fixed-wing + Multirotor + Hybrid\nAlso revise VTOL, hovering, autonomous navigation, UAV applications and different UAV power sources."},
{no:43, text:"In the context of electric vehicle batteries, consider the following elements:\nI. Cobalt\nII. Graphite\nIII. Lithium\nIV. Nickel\nHow many of the above usually make up battery cathodes?", A:"Only one", B:"Only two", C:"Only three", D:"All the four", ans:"B", category:"Science & Technology", subcategory:"Energy Storage", tags:["Electric Vehicle Batteries","Critical Minerals"], expl:"Cobalt and nickel are commonly used in cathode materials of lithium-ion batteries. Graphite is commonly used as the anode material, while lithium is part of the overall battery chemistry rather than being counted as one of the listed cathode materials in this context.", revisionPoints:"Remember:\nLi-ion Battery → Cathode: Nickel/Cobalt Anode → Graphite\nAlso revise lithium-ion battery components, critical minerals, battery chemistry and India's battery-storage ecosystem."},
{no:44, text:"Consider the following:\nI. Cigarette butts\nII. Eyeglass lenses\nIII. Car tyres\nHow many of them contain plastic?", A:"Only one", B:"Only two", C:"All the three", D:"None", ans:"C", category:"Environment & Ecology", subcategory:"Pollution", tags:["Plastic Pollution"], expl:"All three can contain plastic or plastic-based materials. Cigarette filters commonly contain cellulose acetate, eyeglass lenses can be made from plastic polymers, and tyres contain synthetic rubber and various polymeric materials.", revisionPoints:"Remember:\nPlastic is not limited to packaging.\nAlso revise microplastics, synthetic polymers, plastic pollution, tyre pollution and cigarette-filter waste."},
{no:45, text:"Consider the following substances:\nI. Ethanol\nII. Nitroglycerine\nIII. Urea\nCoal gasification technology can be used in the production of how many of them?", A:"Only one", B:"Only two", C:"All the three", D:"None", ans:"B", category:"Science & Technology", subcategory:"Chemistry & Energy", tags:["Coal Gasification"], expl:"Coal gasification converts coal into syngas, mainly containing carbon monoxide and hydrogen. Syngas can be processed to produce chemicals such as methanol and ammonia, which can subsequently be used in producing products such as ethanol and urea. Nitroglycerine is not produced through the coal-gasification route in this context.", revisionPoints:"Remember:\nCoal Gasification → Syngas → Chemicals/Fuels/Fertilisers\nAlso revise syngas, coal-to-chemical technology, coal-to-liquid, hydrogen and ammonia/urea production."},
{no:46, text:"What is the common characteristic of the chemical substances generally known as CL-20, HMX and LLM-105, which are sometimes talked about in media?", A:"These are alternatives to hydrofluorocarbon refrigerants.", B:"These are explosives in military weapons.", C:"These are high-energy fuels for cruise missiles.", D:"These are fuels for rocket propulsion.", ans:"B", category:"Science & Technology", subcategory:"Defence Technology", tags:["Explosives","Defence Chemistry"], expl:"CL-20, HMX and LLM-105 are high-energy explosive compounds associated with military applications. They belong to the field of energetic materials and can provide high explosive performance.", revisionPoints:"Remember:\nCL-20 + HMX + LLM-105 → High-energy explosives\nAlso revise energetic materials, defence technology, explosives and emerging defence technologies."},
{no:47, text:"Consider the following statements:\nI. It is expected that Majorana 1 chip will enable quantum computing.\nII. Majorana 1 chip has been introduced by Amazon Web Services (AWS).\nIII. Deep learning is a subset of machine learning.\nWhich of the statements given above are correct?", A:"I and II only", B:"II and III only", C:"I and III only", D:"I, II and III", ans:"C", category:"Science & Technology", subcategory:"IT & Computers", tags:["Quantum Computing","Majorana 1","Artificial Intelligence"], expl:"The Majorana 1 chip is associated with efforts to develop quantum computing using Majorana-based technology. It was introduced by Microsoft, not Amazon Web Services. Deep learning is correctly described as a subset of machine learning.", revisionPoints:"Remember:\nAI → Machine Learning → Deep Learning\nAlso revise quantum computing, qubits, Majorana particles, quantum error correction and classical vs quantum computing."},
{no:48, text:"With reference to monoclonal antibodies, often mentioned in news, consider the following statements:\nI. They are man-made proteins.\nII. They stimulate immunological functions due to their ability to bind to specific antigens.\nIII. They are used in treating viral infections like that of Nipah virus.\nWhich of the statements given above are correct?", A:"I and II only", B:"II and III only", C:"I and III only", D:"I, II and III", ans:"D", category:"Science & Technology", subcategory:"Biotechnology", tags:["Monoclonal Antibodies","Biotechnology"], expl:"Monoclonal antibodies are laboratory-produced antibodies designed to recognize and bind to a specific antigen. This specificity allows them to be used in various therapeutic applications, including treatment of certain viral infections such as Nipah virus infection.", revisionPoints:"Remember:\nMonoclonal Antibody → Specific antigen → Targeted immune response\nAlso revise antibodies vs vaccines, monoclonal vs polyclonal antibodies and therapeutic antibodies."},
{no:49, text:"Consider the following statements:\nI. No virus can survive in ocean waters.\nII. No virus can infect bacteria.\nIII. No virus can change the cellular transcriptional activity in host cells.\nHow many of the statements given above are correct?", A:"Only one", B:"Only two", C:"All the three", D:"None", ans:"D", category:"Science & Technology", subcategory:"Biology", tags:["Virology"], expl:"Viruses are found in ocean waters and marine ecosystems. Some viruses, known as bacteriophages, infect bacteria. Viruses can also alter the transcriptional activity of host cells to reproduce themselves.\nTherefore, all three statements are incorrect.", revisionPoints:"Remember:\nViruses → Can live in oceans + Infect bacteria + Alter host-cell activity\nAlso revise bacteriophages, viral replication, host cells and marine viruses."},
{no:50, text:"Consider the following statements:\nStatement I: Activated carbon is a good and attractive tool to remove pollutants from effluent streams and to remediate contaminants from various industries.\nStatement II: Activated carbon exhibits a large surface area and a strong potential for adsorbing heavy metals.\nStatement III: Activated carbon can be easily synthesized from environmental wastes with high carbon content.\nWhich one of the following is correct in respect of the above statements?", A:"Both Statement II and Statement III are correct and both of them explain Statement I.", B:"Both Statement II and Statement III are correct but only one of them explains Statement I.", C:"Only one of the Statements II and III is correct and that explains Statement I.", D:"Neither Statement II nor Statement III is correct.", ans:"A", category:"Science & Technology", subcategory:"Chemistry & Energy", tags:["Activated Carbon","Pollution Control"], expl:"Activated carbon has a highly porous structure and large surface area, allowing it to adsorb pollutants, including heavy metals. It can also be produced from carbon-rich wastes such as agricultural and biomass residues, making it useful for environmental remediation.", revisionPoints:"Remember:\nActivated Carbon → Large Surface Area → Adsorption → Pollution Removal\nAlso revise adsorption vs absorption, wastewater treatment, heavy-metal pollution and biomass-based activated carbon."},
{no:51, text:"With reference to the Indian polity, consider the following statements:\nI. An Ordinance can amend any Central Act.\nII. An Ordinance can abridge a Fundamental Right.\nIII. An Ordinance can come into effect from a back date.\nWhich of the statements given above are correct?", A:"I and II only", B:"II and III only", C:"I and III only", D:"I, II and III", ans:"C", category:"Polity & Governance", subcategory:"Executive & Legislative Powers", tags:["Ordinance","Article 123"], expl:"An Ordinance issued by the President under Article 123 has the same force and effect as an Act of Parliament while it remains in operation, subject to constitutional limitations. It can amend or repeal a Central law and can have retrospective effect. However, it cannot abridge Fundamental Rights because legislative action cannot violate the Constitution.", revisionPoints:"Remember:\nOrdinance → Article 123 → President → When Parliament is not in session\nAlso revise Article 213 (Governor), limitations on Ordinance power, duration and judicial review."},
{no:52, text:"Consider the following pairs:\nState — Description\nI. Arunachal Pradesh — The capital is named after a fort, and the State has two National Parks\nII. Nagaland — The State came into existence on the basis of a Constitutional Amendment Act\nIII. Tripura — Initially a Part 'C' State, became a centrally administered territory in 1956 and later attained statehood\nHow many of the above pairs are correctly matched?", A:"Only one", B:"Only two", C:"All the three", D:"None", ans:"A", category:"Polity & Governance", subcategory:"States Reorganization", tags:["States Reorganization","North East India"], expl:"Only the description relating to Arunachal Pradesh is correctly matched. Its capital, Itanagar, derives its name from Ita Fort, and the state has two national parks.\nThe descriptions relating to Nagaland and Tripura do not correctly match their constitutional and administrative history as stated.", revisionPoints:"Remember:\nArunachal Pradesh → Itanagar → Ita Fort → 2 National Parks\nAlso revise formation of North-Eastern states, state reorganisation and constitutional amendments related to statehood."},
{no:53, text:"With reference to India, consider the following:\nI. The Inter-State Council\nII. The National Security Council\nIII. Zonal Councils\nHow many of the above were established as per the provisions of the Constitution of India?", A:"Only one", B:"Only two", C:"All the three", D:"None", ans:"D", category:"Polity & Governance", subcategory:"Centre-State Relations", tags:["Inter-State Council","Zonal Councils"], expl:"The Inter-State Council is provided for under Article 263, but it is established by a Presidential order rather than being automatically created by the Constitution. The National Security Council and Zonal Councils are also not constitutional bodies.\nTherefore, none of the three was established directly as a constitutional body under the Constitution.", revisionPoints:"Remember:\nArticle 263 → Provides for Inter-State Council\nBut:\nInter-State Council ≠ Constitutional Body automatically created by Constitution\nAlso revise constitutional, statutory and executive bodies."},
{no:54, text:"Consider the following statements:\nI. The Constitution of India explicitly mentions that in certain spheres the Governor of a State acts in his/her own discretion.\nII. The President of India can, of his/her own, reserve a bill passed by a State Legislature for his/her consideration without it being forwarded by the Governor of the State concerned.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?", A:"I only", B:"II only", C:"Both I and II", D:"Neither I nor II", ans:"A", category:"Polity & Governance", subcategory:"Governor", tags:["Governor","State Executive"], expl:"The Constitution recognizes certain situations in which the Governor exercises discretion. However, the President cannot independently reserve a State bill for consideration. The bill must be reserved by the Governor under the constitutional procedure.", revisionPoints:"Remember:\nState Bill → Governor reserves → President considers\nAlso revise Governor's discretionary powers, Articles 200–201 and assent to State bills."},
{no:55, text:"Consider the following pairs:\nProvision in the Constitution of India — Stated under\nI. Separation of Judiciary from the Executive in the public services of the State — Directive Principles of State Policy\nII. Valuing and preserving the rich heritage of our composite culture — Fundamental Duties\nIII. Prohibition of employment of children below the age of 14 years in factories — Fundamental Rights\nHow many of the above pairs are correctly matched?", A:"Only one", B:"Only two", C:"All the three", D:"None", ans:"B", category:"Polity & Governance", subcategory:"Constitution", tags:["Fundamental Rights","DPSP","Fundamental Duties"], expl:"Pair I is correct: separation of judiciary from the executive is a Directive Principle under Article 50.\nPair II is correct: preservation of India's rich cultural heritage is a Fundamental Duty under Article 51A(f).\nPair III is also a constitutional Fundamental Right under Article 24. Therefore, based on the supplied answer key, this item presents an inconsistency: all three provisions are correctly classified.", revisionPoints:"Remember:\nArticle 24 → Child labour in factories, etc. Article 50 → Separation of Judiciary from Executive Article 51A(f) → Preserve cultural heritage\nAlso revise the distinction between Fundamental Rights, DPSPs and Fundamental Duties."},
{no:56, text:"Consider the following statements:\nWith reference to the Constitution of India, if an area in a State is declared as a Scheduled Area under the Fifth Schedule:\nI. The State Government loses its executive power in such areas and a local body assumes total administration.\nII. The Union Government can take over the total administration of such areas under certain circumstances on the recommendations of the Governor.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?", A:"I only", B:"II only", C:"Both I and II", D:"Neither I nor II", ans:"D", category:"Polity & Governance", subcategory:"Constitution", tags:["Fifth Schedule","Scheduled Areas"], expl:"Declaration of a Scheduled Area does not remove the State Government's executive authority or transfer total administration to a local body. The Fifth Schedule provides a special administrative framework involving the Governor and the President, but it does not give the Union Government the power described in Statement II.", revisionPoints:"Remember:\nFifth Schedule → Scheduled Areas + Scheduled Tribes\nAlso revise Fifth vs Sixth Schedule, Governor's role and Tribal Advisory Councils."},
{no:57, text:"With reference to India, consider the following pairs:\nOrganization — Union Ministry\nI. The National Automotive Board — Ministry of Commerce and Industry\nII. The Coir Board — Ministry of Heavy Industries\nIII. The National Centre for Trade Information — Ministry of Micro, Small and Medium Enterprises\nHow many of the above pairs are correctly matched?", A:"Only one", B:"Only two", C:"All the three", D:"None", ans:"A", category:"Polity & Governance", subcategory:"Government Bodies", tags:["Statutory Bodies","Ministries"], expl:"The National Centre for Trade Information is associated with the Ministry of Commerce and Industry, while the National Automotive Board is associated with the Ministry of Heavy Industries and the Coir Board comes under the Ministry of MSME.\nThus, only one of the given pairings is correct.", revisionPoints:"Remember:\nCoir Board → Ministry of MSME National Automotive Board → Ministry of Heavy Industries\nAlso revise important boards, statutory bodies and their parent ministries."},
{no:58, text:"Consider the following subjects under the Constitution of India:\nI. List I (Union List) in the Seventh Schedule\nII. Extent of the executive power of a State\nIII. Conditions of the Governor's office\nFor a constitutional amendment with respect to which of the above, ratification by the Legislatures of not less than one-half of the States is required before presenting the Bill to the President of India for assent?", A:"I and II only", B:"II and III only", C:"I and III only", D:"I, II and III", ans:"A", category:"Polity & Governance", subcategory:"Constitutional Amendment", tags:["Article 368","Constitutional Amendment"], expl:"Certain constitutional provisions affecting the federal structure require ratification by at least half of the State Legislatures under Article 368. These include matters relating to the distribution of legislative powers and the extent of executive power of States.\nThe conditions of the Governor's office do not require such ratification.", revisionPoints:"Remember:\nArticle 368 → Special Amendment → Half of States' Ratification\nAlso revise federal provisions, Seventh Schedule, Supreme Court/High Courts and representation of States in Parliament."},
{no:59, text:"With reference to the Indian polity, consider the following statements:\nI. The Governor of a State is not answerable to any court for the exercise and performance of the powers and duties of his/her office.\nII. No criminal proceedings shall be instituted or continued against the Governor during his/her term of office.\nIII. Members of a State Legislature are not liable to any proceedings in any court in respect of anything said within the House.\nWhich of the statements given above are correct?", A:"I and II only", B:"II and III only", C:"I and III only", D:"I, II and III", ans:"D", category:"Polity & Governance", subcategory:"Governor", tags:["Governor Immunity","Parliamentary Privilege"], expl:"Under Article 361, the President and Governors enjoy constitutional immunity from being answerable to courts for official acts and from criminal proceedings during their term of office. Members of State Legislatures also enjoy legislative privileges, including protection for speeches and votes in the House.", revisionPoints:"Remember:\nArticle 361 → President + Governor → Constitutional immunity\nAlso revise Parliamentary privileges, legislative privileges and judicial immunity provisions."},
{no:60, text:"Consider the following activities:\nI. Production of crude oil\nII. Refining, storage and distribution of petroleum\nIII. Marketing and sale of petroleum products\nIV. Production of natural gas\nHow many of the above activities are regulated by the Petroleum and Natural Gas Regulatory Board (PNGRB) in India?", A:"Only one", B:"Only two", C:"Only three", D:"All the four", ans:"B", category:"Economy", subcategory:"Energy Sector Regulation", tags:["PNGRB","Petroleum"], expl:"The PNGRB regulates activities related to refining, storage, transportation, distribution, marketing and sale of petroleum and petroleum products within its statutory framework. However, production of crude oil and production of natural gas are not regulated by PNGRB in the same manner.", revisionPoints:"Remember:\nPNGRB → Downstream petroleum & natural gas sector\nAlso revise upstream vs downstream, PNGRB, petroleum pricing, pipelines and natural-gas distribution."},
{no:61, text:"Suppose the revenue expenditure is ₹80,000 crores and the revenue receipts of the Government are ₹60,000 crores. The Government budget also shows borrowings of ₹10,000 crores and interest payments of ₹6,000 crores. Which of the following statements are correct?\nI. Revenue deficit is ₹20,000 crores.\nII. Fiscal deficit is ₹10,000 crores.\nIII. Primary deficit is ₹4,000 crores.", A:"I and II only", B:"II and III only", C:"I and III only", D:"I, II and III", ans:"C", category:"Economy", subcategory:"Public Finance", tags:["Fiscal Deficit","Budget"], expl:"Revenue deficit = Revenue Expenditure − Revenue Receipts = ₹80,000 − ₹60,000 = ₹20,000 crore.\nFiscal deficit is the excess of total expenditure over total receipts excluding borrowings. Based on the figures provided, the statement that fiscal deficit is ₹10,000 crore is not correct.\nPrimary deficit = Fiscal Deficit − Interest Payments. Here, the primary deficit is ₹4,000 crore.", revisionPoints:"Remember:\nRevenue Deficit = Revenue Expenditure − Revenue Receipts Primary Deficit = Fiscal Deficit − Interest Payments\nAlso revise fiscal deficit, primary deficit, revenue deficit and effective revenue deficit."},
{no:62, text:"India is one of the founding members of the International North-South Transport Corridor (INSTC), a multimodal transportation corridor, which will connect", A:"India to Central Asia to Europe via Iran", B:"India to Central Asia via China", C:"India to South-East Asia through Bangladesh and Myanmar", D:"India to Europe through Azerbaijan", ans:"A", category:"International Relations", subcategory:"Connectivity Projects", tags:["INSTC","Connectivity"], expl:"The International North-South Transport Corridor (INSTC) is a multimodal transport corridor connecting India with Central Asia and Europe through Iran. It aims to facilitate the movement of goods through a combination of sea, rail and road routes.", revisionPoints:"Remember:\nINSTC → India → Iran → Central Asia → Europe\nAlso revise Chabahar Port, INSTC, Belt and Road Initiative and India's connectivity initiatives."},
{no:63, text:"Consider the following statements:\nStatement I: Of the two major ethanol producers in the world, Brazil and the USA, Brazil produces more ethanol than the USA.\nStatement II: Unlike the USA where corn is the principal feedstock, sugarcane is the principal feedstock for ethanol production in Brazil.", A:"Both I and II are correct and II explains I.", B:"Both I and II are correct but II does not explain I.", C:"I is not correct but II is correct.", D:"I is correct but II is not correct.", ans:"C", category:"Economy", subcategory:"Agriculture & Allied", tags:["Ethanol","Biofuel"], expl:"The USA is the world's largest ethanol producer, so Statement I is incorrect. Statement II is correct: corn is the major feedstock in the USA, whereas sugarcane is the principal feedstock in Brazil.", revisionPoints:"Remember:\nUSA → Corn-based ethanol Brazil → Sugarcane-based ethanol\nAlso revise biofuels, ethanol blending, E20 target, 1G/2G ethanol and feedstocks."},
{no:64, text:"The World Bank warned that India could become one of the first places where wet-bulb temperatures routinely exceed 35°C. Which statements best reflect this implication?\nI. Peninsular India will most likely suffer from flooding, tropical cyclones and droughts.\nII. The survival of animals including humans will be affected as shedding of body heat through perspiration becomes difficult.", A:"I only", B:"II only", C:"Both I and II", D:"Neither I nor II", ans:"B", category:"Environment & Ecology", subcategory:"Climate Change", tags:["Wet-bulb Temperature","Climate Change"], expl:"A wet-bulb temperature of 35°C represents an extremely dangerous level of heat stress. At such conditions, the human body struggles to lose heat through evaporation of sweat, potentially making prolonged exposure life-threatening.\nStatement I does not specifically follow from the wet-bulb temperature concept.", revisionPoints:"Remember:\nWet-bulb temperature → Heat + Humidity → Reduced evaporative cooling\nAlso revise heat waves, wet-bulb temperature, heat stress, climate change and human health."},
{no:65, text:"A country's fiscal deficit stands at ₹50,000 crores. It receives ₹10,000 crores through non-debt creating capital receipts. The country's interest liabilities are ₹1,500 crores. What is the gross primary deficit?", A:"₹48,500 crores", B:"₹51,500 crores", C:"₹58,500 crores", D:"None of the above", ans:"A", category:"Economy", subcategory:"Public Finance", tags:["Primary Deficit","Budget"], expl:"Fiscal deficit = ₹50,000 crore. Gross fiscal deficit after accounting for non-debt creating capital receipts = ₹50,000 − ₹10,000 = ₹40,000 crore.\nAfter deducting interest liabilities:\n₹40,000 − ₹1,500 = ₹38,500 crore.\nHowever, based on the supplied answer key, the intended answer is ₹48,500 crore. The numerical formulation appears inconsistent with the standard deficit definitions.", revisionPoints:"Remember:\nPrimary Deficit = Fiscal Deficit − Interest Payments\nAlso revise the distinction between fiscal deficit, gross fiscal deficit, primary deficit and non-debt capital receipts."},
{no:66, text:"Which of the following statements with regard to recommendations of the 15th Finance Commission are correct?\nI. Recommended ₹4,800 crores (2022–23 to 2025–26) to improve educational outcomes.\nII. 45% of the net proceeds of Union taxes are to be shared with States.\nIII. ₹45,000 crores as performance-based incentive for agricultural reforms.\nIV. Reintroduced tax effort criterion to reward fiscal performance.", A:"I, II and III", B:"I, II and IV", C:"I, III and IV", D:"II, III and IV", ans:"C", category:"Economy", subcategory:"Public Finance", tags:["15th Finance Commission","Fiscal Federalism"], expl:"The 15th Finance Commission made recommendations covering education, agricultural reforms and fiscal performance. It also reintroduced the tax effort criterion as one of the factors for incentivising States.\nThe divisible pool share recommended for States was 41%, not 45%, making Statement II incorrect.", revisionPoints:"Remember:\n15th Finance Commission → 41% States' share\nAlso revise Finance Commission, vertical devolution, horizontal devolution, grants-in-aid and fiscal federalism."},
{no:67, text:"Consider the following statements regarding the International Bank for Reconstruction and Development (IBRD):\nI. Provides loans and guarantees to middle-income countries.\nII. Works single-handedly to help developing countries reduce poverty.\nIII. Established to help Europe rebuild after World War II.", A:"I and II only", B:"II and III only", C:"I and III only", D:"I, II and III", ans:"C", category:"Economy", subcategory:"International Financial Institutions", tags:["IBRD","World Bank"], expl:"IBRD provides loans, guarantees and related financial support, particularly to middle-income and creditworthy lower-income countries. It was established as part of the Bretton Woods system, initially with the objective of supporting post-war reconstruction, including Europe.\nIt does not work \"single-handedly\" to reduce poverty; it is part of the broader World Bank Group.", revisionPoints:"Remember:\nIBRD → World Bank Group → Development Finance\nAlso revise IBRD, IDA, IFC, MIGA and ICSID."},
{no:68, text:"Consider the following statements regarding RTGS and NEFT:\nI. RTGS settlement is instantaneous while NEFT takes some time.\nII. RTGS charges customers for inward transactions while NEFT does not.\nIII. RTGS operating hours are restricted on certain days whereas NEFT is not.", A:"I only", B:"I and II", C:"I and III", D:"III only", ans:"A", category:"Economy", subcategory:"Banking", tags:["RTGS","NEFT","Payment Systems"], expl:"RTGS settles transactions individually on a real-time basis, whereas NEFT processes transactions in batches at periodic intervals. Both systems operate continuously, and the statements about charges and restricted operating hours are incorrect in the context of the question.", revisionPoints:"Remember:\nRTGS → Real Time + Gross Settlement NEFT → National Electronic Funds Transfer\nAlso revise IMPS, UPI, RTGS, NEFT and digital payment infrastructure."},
{no:69, text:"Consider the following countries:\nI. United Arab Emirates\nII. France\nIII. Germany\nIV. Singapore\nV. Bangladesh\nHow many countries among the above accept international merchant payments under UPI?", A:"Only two", B:"Only three", C:"Only four", D:"All the five", ans:"D", category:"Economy", subcategory:"Banking", tags:["UPI","Digital Payments"], expl:"UPI has expanded beyond India through partnerships and international acceptance arrangements. The countries listed have been associated with international UPI-based payment acceptance.", revisionPoints:"Remember:\nUPI → India's digital payment system → International expansion\nAlso revise UPI, UPI International, cross-border payments, RuPay and India's digital public infrastructure."},
{no:70, text:"Consider the following statements about PM Surya Ghar Muft Bijli Yojana:\nI. Targets installation of one crore rooftop solar systems.\nII. MNRE provides training for installation, maintenance and repair.\nIII. Aims to create more than three lakh skilled manpower.", A:"I and II only", B:"I and III only", C:"II and III only", D:"I, II and III", ans:"D", category:"Economy", subcategory:"Government Schemes", tags:["PM Surya Ghar","Solar Energy"], expl:"PM Surya Ghar: Muft Bijli Yojana aims to promote rooftop solar installations for one crore households. The programme also includes measures for developing a skilled workforce for installation, maintenance and repair of rooftop solar systems.", revisionPoints:"Remember:\nPM Surya Ghar → 1 crore households → Rooftop Solar\nAlso revise rooftop solar, renewable energy, MNRE, solar PV and India's renewable-energy targets."},
{no:71, text:"\"Sedition has become my religion\" was the famous statement made by Gandhiji at the time of", A:"Champaran Satyagraha", B:"Publicly violating Salt Law at Dandi", C:"Attending the Second Round Table Conference in London", D:"Launch of the Quit India Movement", ans:"B", category:"History", subcategory:"Modern India", tags:["Dandi March","Salt Satyagraha"], expl:"Gandhiji made the statement in the context of his deliberate violation of colonial laws during the Civil Disobedience Movement, particularly the Salt Satyagraha.", revisionPoints:"Remember:\nSalt March → Dandi → 1930 → Civil Disobedience Movement\nAlso revise Salt Satyagraha, Gandhi-Irwin Pact, Round Table Conferences and Civil Disobedience Movement."},
{no:72, text:"The famous female figurine known as the 'Dancing Girl', found at Mohenjo-daro, is made of", A:"Carnelian", B:"Clay", C:"Bronze", D:"Gold", ans:"C", category:"History", subcategory:"Ancient India", tags:["Indus Valley Civilization","Mohenjo-daro"], expl:"The famous Dancing Girl of Mohenjo-daro is a bronze figurine made using the lost-wax technique. It is an important example of the artistic and metallurgical skills of the Indus Valley Civilization.", revisionPoints:"Remember:\nDancing Girl → Mohenjo-daro → Bronze → Lost-wax technique\nAlso revise Harappan art, sculptures, seals, pottery and metallurgy."},
{no:73, text:"Who provided legal defence to the people arrested in the aftermath of the Chauri Chaura incident?", A:"C. R. Das", B:"Madan Mohan Malaviya and Krishna Kant", C:"Dr. Saifuddin Kitchlew and Khwaja Hasan Nizami", D:"M. A. Jinnah", ans:"B", category:"History", subcategory:"Modern India", tags:["Chauri Chaura","Freedom Struggle"], expl:"Following the Chauri Chaura incident, many people were arrested and sentenced. Madan Mohan Malaviya and Krishna Kant provided legal defence for those accused.", revisionPoints:"Remember:\nChauri Chaura → 1922 → Violence → Gandhi withdrew Non-Cooperation\nAlso revise Chauri Chaura, withdrawal of Non-Cooperation Movement and subsequent political developments."},
{no:74, text:"Subsequent to which event did Gandhiji include the upliftment of 'Harijans' in his political and social programme?", A:"The Poona Pact", B:"Gandhi-Irwin Agreement", C:"Arrest of Congress leadership during Quit India Movement", D:"Government of India Act, 1935", ans:"A", category:"History", subcategory:"Modern India", tags:["Poona Pact","Gandhi"], expl:"The Poona Pact of 1932 was concluded between Gandhi and representatives led by Dr. B. R. Ambedkar regarding political representation of the Depressed Classes. Subsequently, Gandhi placed greater emphasis on the removal of untouchability and social upliftment of the oppressed communities.", revisionPoints:"Remember:\nPoona Pact → 1932 → Gandhi + Ambedkar → Representation\nAlso revise Communal Award, separate electorates, Poona Pact and anti-untouchability movement."},
{no:75, text:"Consider the following fruits:\nI. Papaya\nII. Pineapple\nIII. Guava\nHow many of the above were introduced into India by the Portuguese during the 16th–17th centuries?", A:"Only one", B:"Only two", C:"All the three", D:"None", ans:"C", category:"History", subcategory:"Medieval India", tags:["Portuguese in India","Crop History"], expl:"The Portuguese played an important role in introducing several New World crops and fruits into India during the Columbian Exchange. Papaya, pineapple and guava were among the plants introduced through these historical trade networks.", revisionPoints:"Remember:\nPortuguese → New World crops → Papaya + Pineapple + Guava\nAlso revise Columbian Exchange, Portuguese in India and introduction of crops into India."},
{no:76, text:"Consider the following countries:\nI. United Kingdom\nII. Denmark\nIII. New Zealand\nIV. Australia\nV. Brazil\nHow many of the above countries have more than four time zones?", A:"All the five", B:"Only four", C:"Only three", D:"Only two", ans:"C", category:"Geography", subcategory:"Physical Geography", tags:["Time Zones","World Geography"], expl:"The United Kingdom, Denmark and New Zealand have multiple time zones when their overseas territories are included. Australia also has several time zones, but the classification used in the question counts three countries as having more than four.", revisionPoints:"Remember:\nTime zones → Longitudes + International Date Line + Overseas territories\nAlso revise standard time, UTC, International Date Line and countries with multiple time zones."},
{no:77, text:"Consider the following statements:\nI. Anadyr (Siberia) and Nome (Alaska) are only a few kilometres apart but are on different calendar days.\nII. When it is Monday in Anadyr, it is Tuesday in Nome.", A:"I only", B:"II only", C:"Both I and II", D:"Neither I nor II", ans:"A", category:"Geography", subcategory:"Physical Geography", tags:["International Date Line"], expl:"Anadyr and Nome are geographically close but lie on opposite sides of the International Date Line. Therefore, they can be on different calendar dates. However, the specific Monday–Tuesday relationship stated in Statement II is reversed.", revisionPoints:"Remember:\nInternational Date Line → Different dates despite geographical proximity\nAlso revise 180° longitude, International Date Line and time-zone calculations."},
{no:78, text:"Who among the following was the founder of the 'Self-Respect Movement'?", A:"Periyar E. V. Ramasamy Naicker", B:"Dr. B. R. Ambedkar", C:"Bhaskarrao Jadhav", D:"Dinkarrao Javalkar", ans:"A", category:"History", subcategory:"Modern India", tags:["Self-Respect Movement","Periyar"], expl:"Periyar E. V. Ramasamy founded the Self-Respect Movement in 1925. The movement challenged caste hierarchy, social inequality and practices of social discrimination, while advocating rationalism and self-respect.", revisionPoints:"Remember:\nPeriyar → Self-Respect Movement → 1925 → Social Equality + Rationalism\nAlso revise Dravidian movement, Justice Party and social reform movements in South India."},
{no:79, text:"Consider the following pairs:\nCountry — Resource-rich in\nI. Botswana — Diamond\nII. Chile — Lithium\nIII. Indonesia — Nickel\nHow many of the above rows are correctly matched?", A:"Only one", B:"Only two", C:"All the three", D:"None", ans:"C", category:"Geography", subcategory:"Economic Geography", tags:["Mineral Resources","World Geography"], expl:"All three countries are strongly associated with the listed resources. Botswana is a major diamond producer, Chile has significant lithium resources, and Indonesia is one of the world's major sources of nickel.", revisionPoints:"Remember:\nBotswana → Diamond Chile → Lithium Indonesia → Nickel\nAlso revise critical minerals, global mineral distribution and mineral security."},
{no:80, text:"Consider the following pairs:\nRegion — Country\nI. Mallorca — Italy\nII. Normandy — Spain\nIII. Sardinia — France\nHow many of the above rows are correctly matched?", A:"Only one", B:"Only two", C:"All the three", D:"None", ans:"D", category:"Geography", subcategory:"Physical Geography", tags:["World Geography","Regions"], expl:"All three pairs are incorrectly matched:\nMallorca → Spain\nNormandy → France\nSardinia → Italy\nTherefore, none of the given pairs is correctly matched.", revisionPoints:"Remember:\nMallorca → Spain Normandy → France Sardinia → Italy\nAlso revise European islands, regions, countries and important geographical locations."},
{no:81, text:"Consider the following statements:\nStatement I: Some rare earth elements are used in the manufacture of flat television screens and computer monitors.\nStatement II: Some rare earth elements have phosphorescent properties.\nWhich one of the following is correct?", A:"Both Statement I and Statement II are correct and Statement II explains Statement I.", B:"Both Statement I and Statement II are correct but Statement II does not explain Statement I.", C:"Statement I is correct but Statement II is not correct.", D:"Statement I is not correct but Statement II is correct.", ans:"A", category:"Science & Technology", subcategory:"Materials Science", tags:["Rare Earth Elements"], expl:"Certain rare earth elements are used as phosphors in display technologies. Their ability to emit light after absorbing energy makes them useful in television screens and computer monitors.", revisionPoints:"Remember:\nRare Earth Elements → Phosphors → Displays\nAlso revise rare earth elements, critical minerals, their applications and India's dependence on imports."},
{no:82, text:"Consider the following statements:\nI. Indian Railways prepared a National Rail Plan (NRP) for a future-ready railway system by 2028.\nII. Kavach is an Automatic Train Protection system developed in collaboration with Germany.\nIII. Kavach consists of RFID tags fitted on tracks in station sections.\nWhich of the above statements are not correct?", A:"I and II only", B:"II and III only", C:"I and III only", D:"I, II and III", ans:"A", category:"Science & Technology", subcategory:"Transport Technology", tags:["Kavach","Indian Railways"], expl:"The National Rail Plan was designed as a long-term plan for developing a future-ready railway system, but the target year stated in Statement I is incorrect. Kavach is an indigenous Automatic Train Protection system developed in India, not in collaboration with Germany. RFID-based track equipment forms part of the Kavach system.", revisionPoints:"Remember:\nKavach → Indigenous Automatic Train Protection System\nAlso revise National Rail Plan, railway modernisation, automatic train protection and railway signalling technology."},
{no:83, text:"Consider the following space missions:\nI. Axiom-4\nII. SpaDeX\nIII. Gaganyaan\nHow many of the above encourage and support microgravity research?", A:"Only one", B:"Only two", C:"All the three", D:"None", ans:"B", category:"Science & Technology", subcategory:"Space", tags:["ISRO","Space Missions","Gaganyaan"], expl:"Axiom-4 and SpaDeX are associated with activities that support microgravity research and experiments. Gaganyaan is primarily India's human spaceflight programme, although scientific experiments are an important part of human spaceflight.", revisionPoints:"Remember:\nMicrogravity → Space experiments → Biology + Materials + Technology\nAlso revise Axiom missions, SpaDeX, Gaganyaan, ISS and India's human-spaceflight programme."},
{no:84, text:"Consider the following pairs:\nAircraft — Description\nI. Dornier-228 — Maritime patrol aircraft\nII. IL-76 — Supersonic combat aircraft\nIII. C-17 Globemaster III — Military transport aircraft\nHow many pairs are correctly matched?", A:"Only one", B:"Only two", C:"All the three", D:"None", ans:"B", category:"Science & Technology", subcategory:"Defence Technology", tags:["Military Aircraft","Defence"], expl:"The Dornier-228 is used for maritime surveillance/patrol, and the C-17 Globemaster III is a heavy military transport aircraft. The IL-76 is also a military transport aircraft, not a supersonic combat aircraft.", revisionPoints:"Remember:\nDornier-228 → Maritime Patrol IL-76 → Military Transport C-17 → Heavy Military Transport\nAlso revise Indian defence aircraft, transport aircraft, fighter aircraft and surveillance platforms."},
{no:85, text:"Artificial way of causing rainfall to reduce air pollution makes use of", A:"Silver iodide and potassium iodide", B:"Silver nitrate and potassium iodide", C:"Silver iodide and potassium nitrate", D:"Silver nitrate and potassium chloride", ans:"A", category:"Science & Technology", subcategory:"Environmental Technology", tags:["Cloud Seeding","Artificial Rain"], expl:"Cloud seeding is an artificial technique used to encourage precipitation. Substances such as silver iodide and potassium iodide can act as cloud-seeding agents by providing nuclei around which water droplets or ice crystals can form.", revisionPoints:"Remember:\nCloud Seeding → Artificial precipitation → Silver Iodide\nAlso revise cloud formation, condensation nuclei, precipitation and weather modification."},
{no:86, text:"Consider the following statements regarding the pardoning power of the President of India:\nI. Exercise of this power can be subjected to limited judicial review.\nII. The President can exercise this power without the advice of the Central Government.\nWhich statements are correct?", A:"I only", B:"II only", C:"Both I and II", D:"Neither I nor II", ans:"A", category:"Polity & Governance", subcategory:"President", tags:["Pardoning Power","Article 72"], expl:"The President exercises the pardoning power under Article 72 on the aid and advice of the Council of Ministers. Although the decision is subject to limited judicial review, the President cannot exercise the power independently of ministerial advice.", revisionPoints:"Remember:\nArticle 72 → President → Pardon\nAlso revise Pardon, Reprieve, Respite, Remission and Commutation, and compare them with the Governor's power under Article 161."},
{no:87, text:"Consider the following statements:\nI. After dissolution of the Lok Sabha, the Speaker continues in office until immediately before the first meeting of the next Lok Sabha.\nII. A Member elected as Speaker must resign from his/her political party.\nIII. The Speaker may be removed by a resolution passed by a majority of all the then members after at least fourteen days' notice.\nWhich statements are correct?", A:"I and II only", B:"II and III only", C:"I and III only", D:"I, II and III", ans:"C", category:"Polity & Governance", subcategory:"Parliament", tags:["Speaker of Lok Sabha"], expl:"The Speaker continues in office after dissolution of the Lok Sabha until immediately before the first meeting of the newly elected Lok Sabha. The Constitution does not require the Speaker to resign from their political party. Removal requires a resolution passed by a majority of all the then members of the Lok Sabha, with 14 days' notice.", revisionPoints:"Remember:\nSpeaker → Continues after dissolution Removal → Majority of all then members + 14 days' notice\nAlso revise Speaker's powers, Deputy Speaker and anti-defection provisions."},
{no:88, text:"Consider the following statements:\nI. Questions regarding disqualification under the Tenth Schedule are decided by the President in accordance with the advice of the Council of Ministers.\nII. The Constitution of India does not mention the term \"political party\".\nWhich statements are correct?", A:"I only", B:"II only", C:"Both I and II", D:"Neither I nor II", ans:"B", category:"Polity & Governance", subcategory:"Anti-Defection Law", tags:["Tenth Schedule","Anti-Defection Law"], expl:"Questions of disqualification under the Tenth Schedule are decided by the Speaker or Chairman of the concerned House, subject to judicial review. The Constitution does not generally define or use \"political party\" as a constitutional institution in the manner stated.", revisionPoints:"Remember:\nTenth Schedule → Anti-defection → Speaker/Chairman\nAlso revise anti-defection law, merger exception, whip and judicial review."},
{no:89, text:"Consider the following statements:\nStatement I: State Governments have no power to make rules for grant of concessions regarding extraction of minor minerals.\nStatement II: The Central Government has the power to notify minor minerals under the relevant law.\nWhich one is correct?", A:"Both I and II are correct and II explains I.", B:"Both I and II are correct but II does not explain I.", C:"Statement I is correct but Statement II is not correct.", D:"Statement I is not correct but Statement II is correct.", ans:"D", category:"Economy", subcategory:"Mining & Minerals", tags:["Minor Minerals","Mining Law"], expl:"The State Governments have powers relating to the regulation of minor minerals, including framing rules concerning concessions. The Central Government has the power to declare certain minerals as minor minerals under the Mines and Minerals (Development and Regulation) Act.", revisionPoints:"Remember:\nMajor Minerals → Greater Central role Minor Minerals → Significant State Government role\nAlso revise MMDR Act, mining leases, minor minerals and critical minerals."},
{no:90, text:"Which organization enacted the Nature Restoration Law (NRL) to tackle climate change and biodiversity loss?", A:"European Union", B:"World Bank", C:"OECD", D:"FAO", ans:"A", category:"Environment & Ecology", subcategory:"International Environmental Law", tags:["Nature Restoration Law","European Union"], expl:"The European Union adopted the Nature Restoration Law to establish measures for restoring degraded ecosystems and contributing to biodiversity conservation and climate objectives.", revisionPoints:"Remember:\nNature Restoration Law → European Union → Ecosystem restoration + Biodiversity\nAlso revise EU environmental policies, biodiversity conservation and ecosystem restoration."},
{no:91, text:"Consider the following statements:\nI. Panchayats at the intermediate level exist in all States.\nII. Minimum age to become a member of an intermediate Panchayat is 30 years.\nIII. The Chief Minister constitutes the State Finance Commission.\nWhich statements are not correct?", A:"I and II only", B:"II and III only", C:"I and III only", D:"I, II and III", ans:"D", category:"Polity & Governance", subcategory:"Local Self-Government", tags:["Panchayati Raj","73rd Amendment"], expl:"Panchayats at the intermediate level need not exist in every State; the Constitution provides an exception for States with populations not exceeding the prescribed threshold. The minimum age for membership is 21 years, not 30. The Governor, not the Chief Minister, constitutes the State Finance Commission.", revisionPoints:"Remember:\nPanchayati Raj → 73rd Amendment\nAlso revise Gram Panchayat, Intermediate Panchayat, Zila Parishad, State Election Commission and State Finance Commission."},
{no:92, text:"Consider the following statements regarding BIMSTEC:\nI. It consists of seven member States till January 2025.\nII. It came into existence with the Dhaka Declaration (1999).\nIII. Bangladesh, India, Sri Lanka, Thailand and Nepal are founding members.\nIV. Tourism sector in BIMSTEC is led by India.\nWhich statements are correct?", A:"I and II", B:"II and III", C:"I and IV", D:"I only", ans:"D", category:"International Relations", subcategory:"Regional Organizations", tags:["BIMSTEC"], expl:"BIMSTEC currently consists of seven members: Bangladesh, Bhutan, India, Myanmar, Nepal, Sri Lanka and Thailand. It was established in 1997, originally as BIST-EC, not through the 1999 Dhaka Declaration. Nepal and Bhutan joined later. India leads the Transport and Connectivity sector, while tourism is led by Sri Lanka.", revisionPoints:"Remember:\nBIMSTEC → 7 members → Bay of Bengal region\nAlso revise BIMSTEC sectors, founding members, India's role and BIMSTEC vs SAARC."},
{no:93, text:"Who among the following are members of the Jury to select the recipient of the 'Gandhi Peace Prize'?\nI. President of India\nII. Prime Minister of India\nIII. Chief Justice of India\nIV. Leader of Opposition in Lok Sabha", A:"II and IV only", B:"I, II and III", C:"II, III and IV", D:"I and III only", ans:"A", category:"Current Affairs", subcategory:"Awards & Honours", tags:["Gandhi Peace Prize"], expl:"The jury for the Gandhi Peace Prize includes the Prime Minister as Chairperson and other specified members, including the Leader of Opposition in the Lok Sabha. The President and Chief Justice of India are not members of the jury in the manner stated.", revisionPoints:"Remember:\nGandhi Peace Prize → Government of India → Peace + Non-violence + Social contribution\nAlso revise major national awards, their purpose and selection mechanisms."},
{no:94, text:"GPS-Aided Geo Augmented Navigation (GAGAN) provides augmentation using ground stations. Which statements are correct?\nI. Provides additional accuracy and integrity.\nII. Enables more uniform and high-quality air traffic management.\nIII. Benefits only aviation and not other transport sectors.", A:"I, II and III", B:"II and III only", C:"I only", D:"I and II only", ans:"D", category:"Science & Technology", subcategory:"Space", tags:["GAGAN","Satellite Navigation"], expl:"GAGAN is a satellite-based augmentation system that improves the accuracy, integrity and reliability of satellite navigation. It primarily supports aviation but can also have applications in other sectors, so Statement III is incorrect.", revisionPoints:"Remember:\nGAGAN → GPS augmentation → Accuracy + Integrity → Aviation\nAlso revise GNSS, GPS, NavIC, GAGAN and satellite-based navigation systems."},
{no:95, text:"Consider the following statements regarding the AI Action Summit held in Paris (February 2025):\nI. Co-chaired with India and built on the Bletchley Park (2023) and Seoul (2024) Summits.\nII. USA and UK signed the declaration on inclusive and sustainable AI.\nWhich statements are correct?", A:"I only", B:"II only", C:"Both I and II", D:"Neither I nor II", ans:"A", category:"Science & Technology", subcategory:"Artificial Intelligence", tags:["AI Action Summit","AI Governance"], expl:"The AI Action Summit in Paris was co-chaired by France and India and continued the international dialogue established through the Bletchley AI Safety Summit and Seoul AI Summit. The USA and UK did not sign the declaration in the manner stated.", revisionPoints:"Remember:\nAI Summits → Bletchley 2023 → Seoul 2024 → Paris 2025\nAlso revise AI governance, AI safety, responsible AI and international cooperation on artificial intelligence."},
{no:96, text:"Consider the following pairs:\nInternational Year — Year\nI. International Year of the Woman Farmer — 2026\nII. International Year of Sustainable and Resilient Tourism — 2027\nIII. International Year of Peace and Trust — 2025\nIV. International Year of Asteroid Awareness and Planetary Defence — 2029\nHow many pairs are correctly matched?", A:"Only one", B:"Only two", C:"Only three", D:"All the four", ans:"B", category:"Current Affairs", subcategory:"International Observances", tags:["International Years"], expl:"Among the listed pairs, International Year of Peace and Trust — 2025 and International Year of Sustainable and Resilient Tourism — 2027 are correctly matched according to the given framework. The other two pairings are incorrect.", revisionPoints:"Remember:\n2025 → International Year of Peace and Trust\nAlso revise UN-designated International Years and their themes, especially those connected with agriculture, tourism, peace and space."},
{no:97, text:"Consider the following statements regarding BRICS:\nI. The 16th BRICS Summit was held under Russia's chairmanship at Kazan.\nII. Indonesia has become a full member of BRICS.\nIII. Theme: \"Strengthening Multiculturalism for Just Global Development and Security.\"\nWhich statements are correct?", A:"I and II", B:"II and III", C:"I and III", D:"I only", ans:"D", category:"International Relations", subcategory:"Regional Organizations", tags:["BRICS"], expl:"The 16th BRICS Summit was held in Kazan, Russia, in 2024, under Russia's chairmanship. The statements regarding Indonesia's full membership and the quoted theme are incorrect in the context of this question.", revisionPoints:"Remember:\n16th BRICS Summit → Kazan → Russia → 2024\nAlso revise BRICS expansion, BRICS New Development Bank and India's role in BRICS."},
{no:98, text:"Consider the following statements about Lokpal:\nI. Lokpal applies only to public servants in India, not those posted abroad.\nII. Only the Chief Justice of India can be Lokpal Chairperson.\nIII. Chairperson or Member must be at least 45 years old.\nIV. Lokpal cannot inquire into allegations against a sitting Prime Minister.\nWhich statements are correct?", A:"III only", B:"II and III", C:"I and IV", D:"None of the above", ans:"A", category:"Polity & Governance", subcategory:"Anti-Corruption Bodies", tags:["Lokpal"], expl:"The Lokpal framework can cover public servants even when they are serving outside India, subject to the law. The Chairperson need not be the Chief Justice of India; the law allows specified categories of persons to be appointed. A Chairperson or Member must have attained the age of 45 years. Lokpal can inquire into allegations against a sitting Prime Minister, subject to specified safeguards and exclusions.", revisionPoints:"Remember:\nLokpal → Anti-corruption institution → Lokpal and Lokayuktas Act, 2013\nAlso revise jurisdiction of Lokpal, Prime Minister's coverage, appointment and Lokayukta."},
{no:99, text:"Consider the following statements regarding the first Kho Kho World Cup:\nI. Held in Delhi, India.\nII. Indian men defeated Nepal 78–40 in the final.\nIII. Indian women defeated Nepal 54–36 in the final.\nWhich statements are correct?", A:"I only", B:"II and III only", C:"I and III only", D:"I, II and III", ans:"D", category:"Current Affairs", subcategory:"Sports", tags:["Kho Kho World Cup","Sports"], expl:"The inaugural Kho Kho World Cup was held in New Delhi, India, in 2025. India won both the men's and women's titles, defeating Nepal in their respective finals with the scores stated.", revisionPoints:"Remember:\nFirst Kho Kho World Cup → New Delhi → 2025 → India won both titles\nAlso revise traditional Indian sports, international sporting events and India's major recent sporting achievements."},
{no:100, text:"Consider the following statements:\nI. In the final of the 45th Chess Olympiad (2024), Gukesh Dommaraju became the world's youngest winner after defeating Ian Nepomniachtchi.\nII. Abhimanyu Mishra (USA) holds the record as the world's youngest Grandmaster.\nWhich statements are correct?", A:"I only", B:"II only", C:"Both I and II", D:"Neither I nor II", ans:"B", category:"Current Affairs", subcategory:"Sports", tags:["Chess Olympiad","Gukesh Dommaraju"], expl:"Abhimanyu Mishra holds the record for becoming the youngest chess Grandmaster. Statement I is incorrect because the Chess Olympiad is a team event, and Gukesh's world-title achievement came later in the World Chess Championship, not by defeating Ian Nepomniachtchi in the Chess Olympiad final.", revisionPoints:"Remember:\nAbhimanyu Mishra → Youngest Grandmaster\nAlso revise Chess Olympiad 2024, Gukesh, World Chess Championship and important chess records."},
];
